/**
 * Chinese zodiac guides (/chinese-zodiac/[animal]): one entry per animal in
 * traditional order, with each animal's fixed element. Original Witchy Portal
 * copy in Yulia's voice. Compatibility follows the traditional trine groups,
 * "secret friend" pairs and clash animals six places apart.
 */
export type ChineseAnimal =
  | "Rat" | "Ox" | "Tiger" | "Rabbit" | "Dragon" | "Snake"
  | "Horse" | "Goat" | "Monkey" | "Rooster" | "Dog" | "Pig";

export const CHINESE_ANIMALS: Record<ChineseAnimal, { chineseName: string; keywords: string[]; overview: string; strengths: string; challenges: string; love: string; work: string; compatibility: string; ritual: string }> = {
  Rat: {
    chineseName: "Shǔ 鼠",
    keywords: ["Clever", "Resourceful", "Quick", "Charming", "Alert"],
    overview: `The Rat opens the whole cycle of twelve, and in the old tale of the Great Race it won that place by wit rather than muscle, riding across the river on the Ox's back and leaping ahead at the very last moment. That story tells you a great deal. Rat energy notices what others miss: the open door, the useful friend, the better way round.\n\nIts fixed element is Water, and that suits it perfectly. Water finds every gap, adapts to any shape and keeps moving. People born in a Rat year often have quick minds, sharp instincts and a gift for reading a room within seconds. They tend to be sociable and warm with the people they trust, yet private about their own plans. The Rat likes to know where the exits are, and it likes to have something set aside for winter. That is not fear. It is wisdom from a creature that has survived everywhere.\n\nRemember that the animal year begins at the Lunar New Year, not on the first of January, so check the date if you were born in January or February. In a full Chinese chart your year animal is only one part of the picture, alongside your month, day and hour. Still, the year animal shows the face you carry into the world. For the Rat, that face is bright, curious and ready. Trust your instincts. They are rarely wrong.`,
    strengths: `The Rat is resourceful in a way that feels almost magical. Give it very little and it will make something useful. It is perceptive, quick with words and wonderful at spotting patterns before anyone else does. Rats are often loyal and generous with their inner circle, quietly making sure the people they love are looked after. They adapt to change without fuss, learn fast and keep their sense of humour when things get tense. Their curiosity keeps them young.`,
    challenges: `That busy mind can become restless and anxious, always calculating the next move. The Rat may hold back its true feelings, keeping people guessing, or hold on to resources so tightly that it forgets to enjoy them. Under pressure it can turn sharp-tongued or a little suspicious. My advice: not everything needs a back-up plan. Let yourself rest in what you already have, and share your thoughts before they turn into worries. Openness is also a kind of cleverness.`,
    love: `In love, the Rat is attentive and affectionate once it feels safe, but it rarely rushes. It watches first, gathering small details about a person, and then gives its heart with surprising devotion. Rats remember the little things: the song you mentioned once, the tea you prefer. They show love through care and practical help as much as through words.\n\nIf you are single, Rat energy asks you to let someone see the playful side you usually keep for close friends. If you are in a relationship, the lesson is to share worries early rather than solving everything alone in your head. A partner who can match the Rat's wit and give it a sense of security will find a clever, tender and fiercely loyal companion.`,
    work: `The Rat brings energy, sharp thinking and a nose for opportunity to any kind of work. It does well wherever quick judgement and good networking matter, and it often sees the practical solution while others are still debating. Rats are excellent at managing resources, spotting what is not working and finding a cleverer route. They like progress they can see.\n\nThe shadow is taking on too much at once because every idea looks promising. Choose your best few and finish them. Let colleagues in on your thinking, too, because people trust what they understand. When the Rat combines its talents with patience, it builds something lasting.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Rat shares its trine with the Dragon and the Monkey, three quick, ambitious animals who understand each other's drive and enjoy each other's company. Its secret friend is the Ox, the steady partner from the Great Race, who gives the Rat calm and the Rat gives back spark. The clash animal is the Horse, six places away, whose fiery independence can rub against the Rat's careful planning. A clash is not a closed door. With respect and honest talk, any two animals can build something beautiful.`,
    ritual: `On a Wednesday evening, place a small bowl of moon water beside a blue candle. Drop in three silver coins or clean pebbles and name three things you are grateful to have. Let the candle burn for a while, then pour the water on a plant. Abundance grows when it moves.`,
  },
  Ox: {
    chineseName: "Niú 牛",
    keywords: ["Steady", "Patient", "Reliable", "Strong", "Honest"],
    overview: `The Ox is the second animal of the cycle, and it carries a strength that does not need to shout. In the Great Race it was the Ox that swam the river with steady power, carrying the Rat on its back. It did not win first place, but it never stopped. That is the Ox in one image: patient, dependable and deeply strong.\n\nIts fixed element is Earth, which gives it roots. People born in an Ox year tend to be grounded, practical and honest, with a quiet dignity that others lean on. They prefer real work to empty promises and slow, solid progress to dramatic leaps. The Ox does not change its mind easily, and when it gives its word, it means it. There is something very old and comforting in this energy, like a field that has been tended for generations.\n\nThe animal year begins at the Lunar New Year, so if your birthday falls in January or February, check which side of it you were born on. Your year animal is one layer of a Chinese chart, which also looks at month, day and hour, yet it says much about how you meet the world. The Ox meets it with patience and backbone. Witches understand this energy well, because real magic is often slow magic: the same intention, repeated, until it becomes real. Never doubt your own pace. It is carrying you exactly where you need to go.`,
    strengths: `The Ox is reliable in a world that often is not. It finishes what it starts, keeps its promises and stays calm when others panic. Its patience is a real power, letting it outlast difficulties that would exhaust anyone else. Oxen are honest, sometimes bluntly so, and people trust them for it. They are hardworking, methodical and protective of family and friends. When an Ox builds something, whether a home, a skill or a friendship, it is built to last.`,
    challenges: `Strength can harden into stubbornness. Once the Ox has decided, it may refuse to hear another view, even when the situation has changed. It can also carry too much in silence, never asking for help, until resentment builds up quietly underneath. Change feels uncomfortable, so the Ox may stay too long in what is familiar. Try loosening your grip a little. Bending is not breaking, and asking for support is a sign of wisdom, not weakness.`,
    love: `The Ox loves slowly and deeply. It is not one for grand speeches or whirlwind romance. Instead it shows love by turning up: the repaired shelf, the meal ready when you come home, the steady presence through hard times. Once committed, an Ox is wonderfully loyal and expects loyalty in return.\n\nIf you are single, give new connections time to grow, and do not dismiss someone just because the spark is gentle at first. Your best bonds tend to build like a fire from small logs. If you are in a relationship, practise saying what you feel out loud. Your partner may not always read your quiet care, and a few warm words can open a door. With trust, the Ox becomes the safest harbour anyone could hope for.`,
    work: `At work, the Ox is the person everyone relies on when something truly has to be done properly. It brings focus, endurance and a high standard, and it rarely cuts corners. Oxen thrive with clear goals, solid structure and the freedom to work through a task thoroughly. They are often natural keepers of tradition and quality in any team.\n\nThe lesson is flexibility. New methods can feel like a threat, yet some of them will save you real effort. Stay open to fresh ideas, and let others see your progress along the way. Your patience is rare and valuable. Make sure it is noticed.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Ox belongs to a trine with the Snake and the Rooster, three thoughtful, determined animals who value loyalty and careful effort. Its secret friend is the Rat, whose lively mind brightens the Ox's steady world while the Ox offers calm in return. The clash animal is the Goat, six places away, whose dreamy softness can puzzle the practical Ox. Yet the Goat can teach tenderness and the Ox can teach steadiness. Any pairing can work when both people choose kindness.`,
    ritual: `Fill a small pot with soil and press in a piece of smoky quartz or a few grains of rice. Light a brown or green candle beside it and say what you are building slowly this year. Keep the pot on your windowsill. Touch the soil whenever you need patience.`,
  },
  Tiger: {
    chineseName: "Hǔ 虎",
    keywords: ["Brave", "Passionate", "Bold", "Protective", "Free"],
    overview: `The Tiger is the third animal of the cycle, and few creatures carry so much presence. In many parts of China the tiger was honoured as a guardian against harm, its image painted on doors and stitched onto children's shoes for protection. That protective courage is the heart of Tiger energy. It walks into the room and the air changes.\n\nIts fixed element is Wood, the element of growth, spring and upward movement. People born in a Tiger year often have bold spirits, strong ideals and an instinct to defend what they love. They are drawn to adventure and to causes bigger than themselves. Tigers act on feeling, and their feelings run hot and honest. They would rather try and stumble than never try at all. Life around a Tiger is rarely dull, and those close to them often find themselves braver simply by standing nearby.\n\nThe Tiger year starts at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if you were born in January or February. In a full Chinese chart your year animal sits beside your month, day and hour animals, each adding its own colour. Yet the year animal shows your outward spirit, and the Tiger's spirit is fierce and generous. My only reminder for Tigers is this: courage also means knowing when to rest. Even the greatest hunter sleeps in the shade. Protect your fire, and it will protect you.`,
    strengths: `The Tiger is courageous in the truest sense, willing to stand up for others and to speak when silence would be easier. It is enthusiastic, warm-hearted and inspiring, often the first to start something new. Tigers are fiercely loyal and protective of the people they love. They have a natural charisma and a strong sense of justice. Their energy can lift a whole group, and their honesty means you always know where you stand with them.`,
    challenges: `All that fire can burn too quickly. The Tiger may act before thinking, take risks that were not needed, or struggle with routine and rules. It can be proud and find it hard to admit a mistake. When it feels caged, it becomes restless and short-tempered. Learn to pause before the leap. Don't let impatience decide for you. Channel your intensity into one clear purpose, and your strength becomes unstoppable rather than scattered.`,
    love: `In love, the Tiger is passionate, romantic and wholehearted. When it falls, it falls completely, and it loves with bold gestures and real warmth. Tigers want a connection that feels alive, full of honesty, adventure and shared ideals. They are deeply protective of a partner and will defend them against anything.\n\nIf you are single, let your natural magnetism work for you, but give people time to show who they really are before you leap. If you are in a relationship, keep the adventure alive with new experiences together, and remember that your partner needs space to be heard, not just protected. The Tiger also needs freedom, so the best bonds are those where both people can roam and still come home to each other.`,
    work: `At work, the Tiger is a natural pioneer. It loves a challenge, a new project or a cause it can believe in, and it brings enormous drive once its heart is engaged. Tigers are often good at leading, motivating others and taking decisive action when everyone else hesitates. They do best with some independence and room to move.\n\nRoutine and slow approval processes can frustrate them. Learn to pace yourself and to bring others along rather than charging ahead alone. When you pair your boldness with a little planning, your ideas move from exciting to truly lasting.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Tiger shares a trine with the Horse and the Dog, three spirited, loyal animals who value freedom and honesty. Its secret friend is the Pig, whose warmth and easy trust soften the Tiger's intensity and offer a gentle home. The clash animal is the Monkey, six places away, whose clever playfulness can feel like teasing to the earnest Tiger. With patience, though, the Monkey brings humour and the Tiger brings heart. Every pairing has its gifts when both choose respect.`,
    ritual: `At dawn, light an orange candle and hold a piece of tiger's eye in your palm. Say aloud one thing you will defend this month and one thing you will let rest. Breathe slowly three times. Carry the stone with you as a reminder that true courage is calm.`,
  },
  Rabbit: {
    chineseName: "Tù 兔",
    keywords: ["Gentle", "Graceful", "Kind", "Intuitive", "Elegant"],
    overview: `The Rabbit is the fourth animal of the cycle, and its energy is soft, graceful and quietly wise. In Chinese folklore the Jade Rabbit lives on the Moon, pounding herbs with a pestle, and so this animal has long been linked with moonlight, healing plants and gentle magic. As a witch, I find that image very dear.\n\nIts fixed element is Wood, the energy of spring and new leaves. People born in a Rabbit year often have refined taste, a calm presence and a deep need for harmony. They notice beauty everywhere, from the arrangement of a room to the tone of a voice, and they are sensitive to atmospheres that others barely register. Rabbits tend to be kind, diplomatic and good listeners. They prefer to solve problems with tact rather than force, and they know that a soft word often achieves more than a loud one.\n\nThe Rabbit year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if you were born in January or February. In Vietnam the Cat takes this place in the zodiac, a reminder that traditions travel and change. In a full Chinese chart the year animal is joined by month, day and hour animals, yet it still shows your outer nature. For the Rabbit, that nature is gentle but not weak. Its quietness hides sharp intuition and real resilience. Trust your sensitivity. It is a compass, not a flaw.`,
    strengths: `The Rabbit is gracious, kind and wonderfully perceptive about people. It creates calm wherever it goes and has a talent for making others feel welcome and understood. Rabbits are diplomatic, thoughtful and careful with their words, so they are often the peacemakers in any group. They have an eye for beauty and detail, and their homes tend to feel like sanctuaries. Their intuition is strong, and their quiet resilience is often underestimated.`,
    challenges: `The Rabbit's love of peace can turn into avoiding conflict at any cost. It may say yes when it means no, or retreat rather than face a hard conversation. Sensitivity can tip into worry, and the Rabbit may overthink a small comment for days. It can also cling to comfort and resist change. Don't hide from what needs to be said. Gentle honesty protects your peace far better than silence ever could.`,
    love: `In love, the Rabbit is tender, romantic and attentive. It wants a relationship that feels safe, calm and beautiful, and it creates that atmosphere naturally with thoughtful gestures and a soft touch. Rabbits listen deeply and remember what matters to the people they love. They are loyal and prefer depth to drama.\n\nIf you are single, trust your intuition about people, because it is usually right, and do not settle for someone who unsettles your peace. If you are in a relationship, practise naming your needs instead of hoping your partner will guess them. Small disagreements handled kindly will bring you closer, not push you apart. With the right person, the Rabbit's love is a quiet, glowing light that warms every corner of a home.`,
    work: `At work, the Rabbit shines wherever care, taste and good judgement matter. It is excellent with people, skilled at smoothing tensions and gifted at creating order and beauty out of chaos. Rabbits often work best in calm, respectful environments where their thoughtful approach is valued.\n\nHarsh competition can drain them, and they may hesitate to put themselves forward. Speak up about your ideas, because they are often wiser than you think. Set gentle boundaries around your energy, too. When the Rabbit trusts its own voice, it becomes a quiet leader whom others are glad to follow.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Rabbit shares a trine with the Goat and the Pig, three gentle, warm-hearted animals who value kindness, comfort and harmony. Its secret friend is the Dog, whose honesty and loyalty make the Rabbit feel truly safe. The clash animal is the Rooster, six places away, whose directness and love of precision can feel sharp to the sensitive Rabbit. Yet the Rooster can bring clarity and the Rabbit can bring grace. Any two hearts can meet in the middle with care.`,
    ritual: `On a Full Moon night, place a sprig of lavender and a moonstone on a white saucer by the window. Light a white candle and whisper a wish for peace in your home. Leave the saucer there overnight, then keep the lavender under your pillow for gentle dreams.`,
  },
  Dragon: {
    chineseName: "Lóng 龙",
    keywords: ["Powerful", "Confident", "Visionary", "Generous", "Magnetic"],
    overview: `The Dragon is the fifth animal of the cycle and the only mythical creature among the twelve. In Chinese tradition the dragon is not a monster to be slain, as in many European tales, but a wise and benevolent power linked with rain, rivers and good fortune. It was a symbol of emperors and of the life-giving forces of nature. Dragon years are often welcomed with special excitement.\n\nIts fixed element is Earth, which may surprise people who imagine dragons as pure fire. Earth gives this great creature its centre and its ability to make visions real. People born in a Dragon year often carry natural confidence, big dreams and a magnetic presence. They tend to think on a grand scale and inspire others simply by believing so strongly in what is possible. Dragons are generous, energetic and proud, and they rarely do anything halfway.\n\nThe Dragon year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if you were born in January or February. Your year animal is only one part of a full Chinese chart, which also looks at month, day and hour. Yet the year animal shows your outward fire, and the Dragon's is impossible to miss. Use that power wisely. A true Dragon does not just shine for itself. It brings rain to dry fields and lifts everyone around it. That is the real meaning of its strength.`,
    strengths: `The Dragon is bold, visionary and full of life. It has the confidence to dream big and the energy to chase those dreams with real determination. Dragons are natural leaders who inspire others with their enthusiasm and conviction. They are generous with their time, their ideas and their warmth. Honest and direct, they say what they think. Their charisma opens doors, and their resilience means they rise again after every setback.`,
    challenges: `A Dragon's confidence can slide into pride, making it hard to accept advice or admit it was wrong. It may expect too much of itself and of others, growing impatient when people move slower. Dragons can also take on huge projects and feel frustrated when reality does not match the vision. Let others help you carry the load. Listen as much as you lead. Humility makes a Dragon's power softer, wiser and far more lasting.`,
    love: `In love, the Dragon is passionate, generous and full of grand gestures. It wants a partner who is a true equal, someone with their own fire and ambitions, and it is drawn to people who admire its spirit without trying to dim it. Dragons bring excitement, devotion and bold romance into a relationship.\n\nIf you are single, remember that the most meaningful connection may not arrive with fireworks. Give quieter people a chance to show their depth. If you are in a relationship, make space for your partner's dreams as well as your own, and let them see your softer, more vulnerable side. A Dragon who learns to share the spotlight discovers a love that feels both thrilling and deeply safe.`,
    work: `At work, the Dragon thrives when it can lead, innovate and aim high. It brings enormous vision and the courage to take on what others think impossible. Dragons are often excellent at starting projects, rallying people and making bold decisions under pressure.\n\nThe lesson is patience with detail and with people. Not every task needs to be epic, and not everyone works at your speed. Learn to delegate with trust and to celebrate other people's contributions. When the Dragon combines its big vision with steady follow-through, it creates work that truly changes things.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Dragon shares a trine with the Rat and the Monkey, three ambitious, quick and lively animals who fire each other's imaginations. Its secret friend is the Rooster, whose sharp eye and steady standards help turn the Dragon's visions into something real. The clash animal is the Dog, six places away, whose cautious realism can feel like doubt to the bold Dragon. Yet the Dog offers honesty and the Dragon offers inspiration. With mutual respect, any pair can thrive together.`,
    ritual: `On a rainy day, set a bowl outside to collect rainwater, the Dragon's own gift. Bring it in, light a gold candle beside it and add a single citrine. Speak your biggest dream into the water. Use a few drops to anoint your wrists whenever you need courage.`,
  },
  Snake: {
    chineseName: "Shé 蛇",
    keywords: ["Wise", "Intuitive", "Elegant", "Discreet", "Deep"],
    overview: `The Snake is the sixth animal of the cycle, and it carries an old, deep wisdom. In Chinese tradition the snake is sometimes called the little dragon, and it has long been linked with intelligence, mystery and transformation. A snake sheds its skin and emerges renewed, and that is the great lesson of this animal: you can always begin again, more yourself than before.\n\nIts fixed element is Fire, but this is a quiet, inner fire, like embers glowing beneath the surface rather than open flames. People born in a Snake year often have sharp minds, strong intuition and a calm, composed presence. They think before they speak, notice everything and rarely show all their cards. Snakes are drawn to depth in conversation, in study and in relationships. They have elegant taste and a natural sense of timing, waiting patiently until the right moment and then moving with precision.\n\nThe Snake year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if your birthday is in January or February. People searching for the year of the Snake are often surprised by how much grace this animal carries. In a full Chinese chart, the year animal is joined by your month, day and hour, but it still shows how you present yourself to the world. For the Snake, that is with quiet power. Witches recognise this energy at once. Trust your inner knowing. It sees further than most.`,
    strengths: `The Snake is wise, perceptive and deeply intuitive. It reads people and situations with remarkable accuracy and often knows the truth long before it is spoken. Snakes are thoughtful, calm under pressure and excellent at planning. They have refined taste, a love of learning and a gift for seeing beneath the surface. Discreet and trustworthy, they keep secrets well. Their ability to transform and renew themselves gives them a resilience that is truly rare.`,
    challenges: `Because the Snake keeps so much inside, it can become secretive or hard to reach, leaving others unsure of where they stand. It may hold on to hurt for a long time and find it difficult to forgive. Its careful nature can turn into suspicion or possessiveness when it feels insecure. Let people in a little further. Shed the old skin of past wounds. Your depth is beautiful, and it deserves to be shared with those who earn your trust.`,
    love: `In love, the Snake is magnetic, devoted and intensely loyal. It does not open its heart easily, but when it does, it gives itself completely and expects the same depth in return. Snakes are drawn to partners who are intelligent and emotionally honest, and they value meaningful conversation as much as romance.\n\nIf you are single, trust your instincts about who is genuine, but do not let caution keep every door closed. If you are in a relationship, share your feelings openly, even the uncomfortable ones, so that your partner does not have to guess. Jealousy can creep in when the Snake feels unsure, so speak your fears gently rather than guarding them. With trust, the Snake becomes a profoundly wise and tender companion.`,
    work: `At work, the Snake brings focus, strategy and a calm mind that others find reassuring in a crisis. It excels at research, analysis and any task that rewards careful thought and attention to detail. Snakes often prefer to work quietly and present their results when they are ready.\n\nThey can be reluctant to share unfinished ideas or to delegate. Remember that collaboration can sharpen your work rather than dilute it. Let your colleagues see a little of your process. When the Snake's intelligence is paired with openness, it becomes a trusted adviser and a quietly powerful force.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Snake shares a trine with the Ox and the Rooster, three thoughtful, determined animals who value loyalty and careful effort. Its secret friend is the Monkey, whose cleverness and humour bring lightness to the Snake's depths, while the Snake offers wisdom in return. The clash animal is the Pig, six places away, whose open, trusting nature can seem naive to the guarded Snake. Yet the Pig can teach the Snake to trust and the Snake can teach discernment. Any pair can grow together.`,
    ritual: `On a waning moon, write an old habit or hurt on a slip of paper. Light a red candle and hold a piece of obsidian. Read the words once, then burn the paper safely in a fireproof dish. Scatter the ashes outside. Like the snake, you leave the old skin behind.`,
  },
  Horse: {
    chineseName: "Mǎ 马",
    keywords: ["Free", "Energetic", "Warm", "Adventurous", "Lively"],
    overview: `The Horse is the seventh animal of the cycle, and its spirit is pure movement. For thousands of years horses carried people across the vast lands of China, and in Chinese culture the horse became a symbol of speed, perseverance and success on a long journey. Picture a horse galloping across open grassland with its mane flying in the wind. That is the essence of this animal.\n\nIts fixed element is Fire, and the Horse's fire is bright, warm and impossible to contain. People born in a Horse year often have abundant energy, an adventurous heart and a love of freedom above almost everything. They are sociable, cheerful and quick to laugh, and they bring a sense of excitement wherever they go. Horses prefer action to theory and would rather learn by doing than by reading about it. They are honest and open, and their enthusiasm is contagious.\n\nThe Horse year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if you were born in January or February. Your year animal is one layer of a full Chinese chart that also includes your month, day and hour, yet it shows the spirit you carry outwards. For the Horse, that spirit is joyful and free. The lesson for this animal is balance. Freedom is precious, but so are roots. A wise Horse knows when to gallop and when to graze. Honour both, and you will run far.`,
    strengths: `The Horse is energetic, warm and wonderfully optimistic. It brings life to any gathering and has a natural gift for making people feel lifted. Horses are independent, adaptable and quick to act, so they rarely stay stuck for long. They are honest, open-hearted and generous with their enthusiasm. Their love of adventure leads them to new places and ideas, and their perseverance carries them through long journeys that would tire anyone else.`,
    challenges: `The Horse's love of freedom can make it restless, impatient or reluctant to commit. It may start many things with passion and then lose interest before finishing them. Its quick reactions can lead to hasty words, and it can struggle with routine or anyone who tries to rein it in. Don't run from what feels uncomfortable. Learn to stay with things a little longer. The deepest rewards often come after the first excitement has faded.`,
    love: `In love, the Horse is passionate, playful and full of warmth. It falls quickly and brings a wonderful sense of fun to any relationship. Horses want a partner who is also a friend and an adventure companion, someone who will explore the world with them and never try to cage them.\n\nIf you are single, enjoy meeting new people, but give a promising connection the chance to deepen rather than moving on at the first sign of routine. If you are in a relationship, keep things fresh with shared experiences, and remember that steady presence is also a form of love. Your partner may need reassurance that your love of freedom is not a wish to leave. When the Horse finds someone who gives it both space and home, its love is generous and true.`,
    work: `At work, the Horse brings energy, quick thinking and a can-do attitude that motivates everyone around it. It loves variety, movement and new challenges, and it often does its best work under a bit of pressure. Horses are good communicators and enjoy working with people.\n\nThe challenge is staying the course when a project becomes repetitive. Break long tasks into shorter stages so you can feel progress, and celebrate each one. Ask for roles that give you some freedom. When the Horse channels its fire into a clear goal, it can cover remarkable ground.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Horse shares a trine with the Tiger and the Dog, three spirited, loyal animals who value honesty and freedom. Its secret friend is the Goat, whose gentle creativity and warmth give the restless Horse a soft place to land. The clash animal is the Rat, six places away, whose careful planning can feel restrictive to the free-spirited Horse. Yet the Rat offers foresight and the Horse offers courage. Any two people can make it work with patience and understanding.`,
    ritual: `Before a journey, big or small, light a red candle and hold a piece of carnelian. Say where you are going and what you hope to bring home. Place a pinch of salt in your shoe or bag for protection. Let the candle burn while you prepare.`,
  },
  Goat: {
    chineseName: "Yáng 羊",
    keywords: ["Gentle", "Creative", "Caring", "Peaceful", "Artistic"],
    overview: `The Goat is the eighth animal of the cycle, and it carries a soft, creative and deeply caring energy. You may also see it called the Sheep or the Ram, because the Chinese word yang covers all of these animals, and different translations choose different names. Whatever we call it, its nature stays the same: gentle, artistic and kind.\n\nIts fixed element is Earth, which gives the Goat a love of comfort, home and the natural world. People born in a Goat year often have a rich inner life, a vivid imagination and an eye for beauty in the smallest things. They tend to be compassionate, sensitive to other people's feelings and quick to offer help. Goats are happiest in peaceful surroundings with people they love, and they often express themselves through art, music, cooking, gardening or simply making a space feel lovely. In Chinese culture, the character for this animal also appears in words connected with goodness and beauty, which feels very fitting.\n\nThe Goat year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if your birthday falls in January or February. In a full Chinese chart, your year animal sits beside your month, day and hour, but it still reveals much about your spirit. For the Goat, that spirit is tender and creative. The world needs that softness more than it knows. Don't apologise for feeling deeply. It is your magic.`,
    strengths: `The Goat is kind, gentle and wonderfully creative. It sees beauty where others see nothing, and it has a gift for bringing comfort and warmth to everyone around it. Goats are compassionate listeners and loyal friends, always ready to help. They have strong artistic instincts and a natural sense of style. Calm and peace-loving, they bring harmony to tense situations. Their sensitivity makes them deeply intuitive about people and atmospheres.`,
    challenges: `The Goat's sensitivity can tip into worry or self-doubt, and it may need a lot of reassurance before it feels secure. It can avoid difficult decisions, hoping someone else will make them. Under stress it may withdraw into daydreams or become moody. It sometimes depends on others more than it needs to. Trust your own strength. You are more capable than you believe, and making a choice, even a small one, builds real confidence.`,
    love: `In love, the Goat is romantic, affectionate and deeply caring. It wants a gentle, harmonious relationship filled with tenderness, and it pours a great deal of love into making a partner feel cherished. Goats remember special moments and express their feelings through thoughtful gestures and creative surprises.\n\nIf you are single, look for someone who values your sensitivity rather than one who dismisses it, and trust that you deserve steady kindness. If you are in a relationship, share your needs clearly instead of waiting for your partner to notice. Small conversations held gently will help you feel secure. When the Goat feels truly safe and appreciated, it creates a home life full of warmth, beauty and quiet joy.`,
    work: `At work, the Goat shines in roles that use its creativity, care and eye for detail. It brings imagination and a calming presence to any team, and it often notices how people are feeling before anyone else does. Goats work best in supportive, friendly environments rather than harsh, competitive ones.\n\nThey may hesitate to promote their own ideas or to take the lead. Practise speaking up, even when it feels uncomfortable. Set clear priorities so that your energy is not scattered. When the Goat believes in its own talent, its work becomes something truly special.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Goat shares a trine with the Rabbit and the Pig, three gentle, warm-hearted animals who value kindness and harmony. Its secret friend is the Horse, whose energy and confidence bring adventure and courage to the Goat's life, while the Goat gives the Horse a peaceful home. The clash animal is the Ox, six places away, whose practicality can feel stern to the dreamy Goat. Yet the Ox can teach steadiness and the Goat can teach tenderness. Any pair can bloom with care.`,
    ritual: `On a Friday evening, light a pink candle and place a rose quartz beside a small bowl of honey. Dip a fingertip in the honey and touch it to your lips, saying one kind thing about yourself. Let the candle burn. Sweetness begins with how you speak to yourself.`,
  },
  Monkey: {
    chineseName: "Hóu 猴",
    keywords: ["Clever", "Playful", "Curious", "Inventive", "Witty"],
    overview: `The Monkey is the ninth animal of the cycle, and its energy sparkles with wit, curiosity and mischief. Many people know the Monkey King, Sun Wukong, from the classic novel Journey to the West, a clever, rebellious and endlessly inventive hero who challenged heaven itself before learning wisdom on a long journey. He captures the Monkey spirit beautifully: brilliant, playful and always learning.\n\nIts fixed element is Metal, which gives the Monkey sharpness, precision and quick decision-making. People born in a Monkey year often have agile minds, a talent for solving puzzles and an irresistible sense of humour. They love new ideas, games and challenges, and they learn remarkably fast. Monkeys are sociable and entertaining, able to charm almost anyone, and they rarely stay down for long. Where others see a problem, the Monkey sees an interesting puzzle waiting to be cracked.\n\nThe Monkey year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if you were born in January or February. Your year animal is one part of a full Chinese chart, which also considers month, day and hour, yet it shows the personality you bring into the world. For the Monkey, that personality is bright and lively. The lesson, as for the Monkey King, is to pair cleverness with heart. When your wit serves kindness, it becomes a true gift to everyone around you.`,
    strengths: `The Monkey is intelligent, inventive and wonderfully adaptable. It finds creative solutions to problems that stump others, and it learns new skills with ease. Monkeys are witty, entertaining and full of energy, so people love being around them. They are optimistic and resilient, bouncing back quickly from setbacks. Curious about everything, they keep growing throughout life. Their quick thinking makes them excellent in a crisis, and their playfulness keeps life light.`,
    challenges: `The Monkey's quick mind can get bored easily, leading it to jump from one thing to the next without finishing. Its love of cleverness can turn into showing off or teasing that goes too far. It may bend rules when they feel inconvenient, or struggle to take serious matters seriously. Slow down sometimes. Not every moment needs a joke or a shortcut. Depth and patience will make your brilliance even more powerful.`,
    love: `In love, the Monkey is fun, affectionate and full of surprises. It wants a partner who can keep up with its mind, share its laughter and enjoy a little adventure. Monkeys bring energy and playfulness into a relationship, and they rarely let things become dull.\n\nIf you are single, enjoy the flirtation, but look for someone who interests your heart as well as your mind. A real bond takes more than sparkle. If you are in a relationship, show your partner your sincere side, too, and resist turning every serious talk into a joke. Commitment can feel like a cage at first, but the right person will give you room to be yourself. When the Monkey truly loves, it is loyal, devoted and endlessly delightful.`,
    work: `At work, the Monkey is a quick, creative problem-solver who thrives on variety and challenge. It brings fresh ideas, sharp analysis and a sense of fun that lifts the whole team. Monkeys often do well wherever quick thinking, negotiation or invention are valued.\n\nRepetition can bore them, and they may lose focus on long projects. Set yourself small challenges within routine tasks to keep your mind engaged. Follow through on your promises, because reliability will make others trust your brilliance. When the Monkey pairs talent with persistence, there is almost nothing it cannot achieve.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Monkey shares a trine with the Rat and the Dragon, three quick, ambitious and lively animals who inspire each other. Its secret friend is the Snake, whose calm depth and wisdom balance the Monkey's playful energy, while the Monkey brings lightness to the Snake. The clash animal is the Tiger, six places away, whose earnest intensity can clash with the Monkey's love of play. Yet the Tiger brings heart and the Monkey brings humour. Any two can find harmony with respect.`,
    ritual: `On a Wednesday, light a yellow candle and place a clear quartz beside a notebook. Write a question you have been puzzling over at the top of a page. Hold the crystal, breathe deeply and write whatever comes for five minutes without stopping. Read it back. Your intuition knows.`,
  },
  Rooster: {
    chineseName: "Jī 鸡",
    keywords: ["Honest", "Proud", "Diligent", "Observant", "Confident"],
    overview: `The Rooster is the tenth animal of the cycle, and it greets each day with confidence and clarity. Its morning call wakes the world, and in Chinese tradition the rooster has long been seen as a herald of light, said to chase away the shadows of the night. There is something brave in an animal that announces itself so boldly at dawn.\n\nIts fixed element is Metal, which gives the Rooster precision, discipline and a strong sense of what is right. People born in a Rooster year often have keen eyes for detail, high standards and a love of order. They are hardworking, punctual and honest, sometimes to the point of bluntness. Roosters take pride in their appearance and their work, and they like things done properly. They are also sociable and enjoy good company, often with a sharp sense of humour and plenty of stories to tell.\n\nThe Rooster year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if your birthday falls in January or February. In a full Chinese chart, your year animal is joined by your month, day and hour, yet it still shows how you meet the world. For the Rooster, that is with courage and candour. The lesson is softness. Your honesty is a gift, and when you deliver it with warmth, people will hear it far more easily. Let your light wake others gently.`,
    strengths: `The Rooster is honest, diligent and remarkably observant. It notices details that others miss and has the discipline to do things thoroughly and well. Roosters are confident, organised and dependable, so people trust them with important tasks. They are brave enough to speak the truth even when it is uncomfortable. Practical and resourceful, they are excellent at planning. Their pride in their work sets a high standard that inspires those around them.`,
    challenges: `The Rooster's high standards can make it critical, of others and of itself. It may point out flaws too quickly or struggle to let small imperfections go. Its directness can sometimes wound without meaning to, and its pride can make apologies difficult. It may also worry about how it is seen. Be gentle with yourself and others. Perfection is not required for something to be good, and kindness makes honesty easier to hear.`,
    love: `In love, the Rooster is loyal, devoted and refreshingly honest. It shows care through practical acts, such as organising, planning and making sure a partner feels supported. Roosters want a relationship built on trust, respect and clear communication, and they are wonderfully dependable once committed.\n\nIf you are single, let people see your warmth and humour, not only your polished side, and try not to judge too quickly from first impressions. If you are in a relationship, balance your helpful suggestions with appreciation, and let your partner know how much you value them. Little words of praise can mean a great deal. When the Rooster softens its edges, its love becomes steady, protective and deeply sincere.`,
    work: `At work, the Rooster is precise, organised and highly capable. It brings discipline, careful planning and a sharp eye for quality to any role. Roosters often excel in work that rewards accuracy, structure and attention to detail, and they take great pride in doing things well.\n\nThey may become frustrated with colleagues who seem careless or disorganised. Try offering guidance with patience, and remember that people learn at different speeds. Share credit generously and praise good effort out loud. When the Rooster combines its excellent standards with encouragement, it becomes a respected and much-loved presence.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Rooster shares a trine with the Ox and the Snake, three thoughtful, determined animals who value loyalty and careful effort. Its secret friend is the Dragon, whose bold vision is beautifully supported by the Rooster's precision, while the Dragon brings excitement in return. The clash animal is the Rabbit, six places away, whose gentle sensitivity can be bruised by the Rooster's directness. Yet the Rabbit teaches grace and the Rooster teaches clarity. Any pair can thrive with patience.`,
    ritual: `At sunrise, open a window and light a white candle. Hold a piece of clear quartz or pyrite and speak one honest intention for the day. Let the morning air move through the room. Blow out the candle and carry the crystal with you as your bright reminder.`,
  },
  Dog: {
    chineseName: "Gǒu 狗",
    keywords: ["Loyal", "Honest", "Faithful", "Protective", "Fair"],
    overview: `The Dog is the eleventh animal of the cycle, and its heart is loyalty itself. Across many cultures, dogs have stood beside people for thousands of years as guardians, companions and friends, and in Chinese tradition the dog is a symbol of faithfulness and protection. If you want to understand Dog energy, think of the friend who arrives at your door the moment you need them.\n\nIts fixed element is Earth, giving the Dog a grounded, steady and practical nature. People born in a Dog year often have a strong sense of fairness, deep honesty and a desire to protect those who cannot protect themselves. They are trustworthy, reliable and devoted to the people and causes they believe in. Dogs value sincerity and can sense insincerity quickly. They may be a little reserved at first, watching carefully before they trust, but once they open their hearts, they stay true for life.\n\nThe Dog year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if you were born in January or February. Many people searching for dog personality traits find themselves nodding at the loyalty and fairness described here. In a full Chinese chart, your year animal sits beside your month, day and hour, yet it shows your outward nature clearly. For the Dog, that nature is faithful and kind. Remember to guard your own heart as carefully as you guard others'. You deserve the same devotion you give.`,
    strengths: `The Dog is loyal, honest and deeply fair. It stands by its friends and family through every storm and is always ready to defend what is right. Dogs are reliable, responsible and sincere, so people trust them completely. They are good listeners and wise counsellors, offering practical help without judgement. Their sense of justice is strong, and they often champion those who are overlooked. Their warmth and devotion make them treasured friends.`,
    challenges: `The Dog's protective nature can turn into worry, imagining problems before they arrive. It may be slow to trust, holding back from new people or situations. Its strong sense of right and wrong can make it judgemental or stubborn when it feels something is unfair. It may also give so much to others that it forgets itself. Let go of what you cannot control. Trust that the world holds good things, too, and look after your own needs.`,
    love: `In love, the Dog is faithful, caring and wholeheartedly committed. It values honesty above all and wants a partner it can trust completely. Dogs show love through steady presence, protective care and quiet acts of devotion. Once they choose someone, they are in it for the long journey.\n\nIf you are single, take the time you need to trust, but do not let past disappointments keep every new person at a distance. Not everyone will let you down. If you are in a relationship, share your worries openly rather than carrying them alone, and let yourself be cared for as much as you care. When the Dog feels secure, its love becomes a deep, sheltering warmth that makes a partner feel truly at home.`,
    work: `At work, the Dog is dependable, conscientious and deeply committed. It brings integrity, fairness and a strong sense of responsibility to any role. Dogs often thrive in work that helps others or upholds a meaningful cause, and colleagues trust them to tell the truth.\n\nThey can become anxious under unclear expectations or when they sense unfairness. Ask questions early, and speak up calmly when something feels wrong. Protect your energy, too, because you may take on more than your share. When the Dog's loyalty is valued, it becomes the backbone of any team.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Dog shares a trine with the Tiger and the Horse, three spirited, loyal animals who value honesty and freedom. Its secret friend is the Rabbit, whose gentleness and kindness soothe the Dog's worries, while the Dog offers the Rabbit safety. The clash animal is the Dragon, six places away, whose grand ambitions can seem unrealistic to the practical Dog. Yet the Dragon brings inspiration and the Dog brings honesty. Any pair can build trust with patience and respect.`,
    ritual: `On a Saturday evening, sprinkle a line of salt across your doorstep. Light a black or brown candle inside and hold a piece of black tourmaline. Name the people you wish to protect, including yourself. In the morning, sweep the salt outward, away from your home.`,
  },
  Pig: {
    chineseName: "Zhū 猪",
    keywords: ["Generous", "Warm", "Sincere", "Joyful", "Kind"],
    overview: `The Pig is the twelfth and final animal of the cycle, and it closes the circle with warmth, generosity and joy. In the old story of the Great Race, the Pig arrived last because it stopped along the way to eat and rest, and I have always loved that detail. The Pig knows something many of us forget: life is meant to be enjoyed, not only rushed through.\n\nIts fixed element is Water, giving the Pig emotional depth, kindness and a flowing, easy nature. People born in a Pig year often have big hearts, a love of comfort and a genuine wish to see others happy. They are sincere, trusting and generous, quick to share whatever they have. Pigs tend to be easy-going and peaceful, but they are also hardworking when it matters, and they enjoy the rewards of their efforts with real pleasure. In Chinese tradition the pig is linked with abundance and contentment, a symbol of a full and happy home.\n\nThe Pig year begins at the Lunar New Year, so check the date if your birthday falls in January or February. In a full Chinese chart your year animal is joined by month, day and hour animals, yet it still reveals your outer spirit. For the Pig, that spirit is open-hearted and kind. The lesson is discernment. Your trust is a beautiful gift, so give it to those who honour it. Keep your joy, and protect it.`,
    strengths: `The Pig is generous, warm-hearted and truly sincere. It gives freely, forgives easily and brings comfort and good cheer wherever it goes. Pigs are honest and straightforward, with no hidden agendas. They are tolerant, patient and accepting of others' flaws. Hardworking when needed, they also know how to rest and enjoy life's pleasures. Their optimism and kindness create a welcoming atmosphere that draws people close, and a Pig's laughter is often the warmest sound in the room.`,
    challenges: `The Pig's trusting nature can leave it open to being taken advantage of. It may find it hard to say no, giving too much of its time and energy. Its love of comfort can tip into indulgence or avoiding challenges. It may also avoid conflict, letting problems grow. Learn to set gentle boundaries. Your kindness is precious, so protect it. Saying no to some things means saying yes to what truly matters.`,
    love: `In love, the Pig is affectionate, devoted and deeply romantic. It wants a warm, cosy relationship full of shared pleasures, laughter and tenderness. Pigs give generously to their partners and love to create a comfortable home where everyone feels welcome.\n\nIf you are single, enjoy getting to know people, but take time to see whether they are as sincere as you are before you give your whole heart. If you are in a relationship, speak up about your needs rather than always putting your partner first. Your feelings matter just as much. When the Pig finds someone who returns its warmth, the relationship becomes a joyful, nurturing place where love grows richer with time.`,
    work: `At work, the Pig is cooperative, diligent and pleasant to be around. It brings warmth, reliability and a team spirit that makes others feel supported. Pigs often thrive in roles that involve caring for people, creating comfort or bringing a group together.\n\nThey may take on too many favours or struggle with office politics. Protect your time, and do not assume everyone shares your good intentions. Ask for the recognition you deserve. When the Pig balances its generosity with healthy boundaries, it becomes a valued and much-loved part of any team.`,
    compatibility: `Traditionally the Pig shares a trine with the Rabbit and the Goat, three gentle, warm-hearted animals who value kindness, harmony and comfort. Its secret friend is the Tiger, whose bold protectiveness gives the Pig courage, while the Pig offers the Tiger warmth and rest. The clash animal is the Snake, six places away, whose guarded, secretive nature can feel unsettling to the open Pig. Yet the Snake teaches discernment and the Pig teaches trust. Any pair can find their way with care.`,
    ritual: `On a Friday, set a small table with bread, a pinch of salt and a green candle. Light it and give thanks for the comforts in your life. Break the bread and share it with someone, or leave a piece outside for the birds. Generosity returns.`,
  },
};
